\title{dmrfit: Scalable Bayesian Inference for Discrete Markov Random Fields}
\description{
Estimation of discrete Markov random fields through the pseudo-likelihood, for both Ising models (Ising (1925) \doi{10.1007/BF02980577}) and ordinal Markov random fields (Marsman et al. (2025) \doi{10.1017/psy.2024.4}). Point estimates, either maximum pseudo-likelihood or maximum a posteriori, are obtained through a trust region optimization algorithm, with robust standard errors from the Godambe-Huber-White (GHW, or sandwich) estimator, for both full and constrained network structures. Bayesian inference corrects the pseudo-posterior through coordinate rescaling (Arena and Marsman (2026) \doi{10.48550/arXiv.2601.17205}), which matches its covariance to the GHW covariance, and samples from it with the Fisher adaptive Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm (Titsias (2024) \doi{10.48550/arXiv.2305.14442}). For comparison, the posterior based on the full likelihood can also be sampled, exactly for small networks or with the double Metropolis-Hastings algorithm (Liang (2010) \doi{10.1080/00949650902882162}). Savage-Dickey Bayes factors test each pairwise interaction, computed either by sampling importance resampling (Skare et al. (2003) \doi{10.1111/1467-9469.00360}) or from the posterior draws.
}
